<!--
	<timing> holds two different kinds of number, and the distinction matters:

		attackDelayMs / shotDelayMs / consumeDelayMs
		        COOLDOWNS. The minimum gap between one action and the next. The
		        action itself happens immediately on the click.
		impactMs
		        A WIND-UP. The delay from the click until the projectile
		        actually leaves: a bow being drawn, a spear wound back. The
		        click still spends the ammo and stamina and starts the
		        animation; only the shot waits. Omit it and the weapon fires
		        inline on the click, which is what every gun does.
		        Must be SHORTER than the weapon's own cooldown or a second shot
		        could start before the first has left; the loader clamps it and
		        warns rather than letting shots stack.

	typeId is what makes a weapon a THROWABLE (3), not impactMs. A throwable is
	spent to use it: it leaves the inventory on release and lands on the ground
	as loot. A bow has a wind-up without being consumed, which is exactly why
	the two are separate fields.

	<remoteTrigger channel="NAME"/> makes an item a DETONATOR. Using it signals
	every object the same player placed whose objects.xml entry carries a
	matching <remote channel="NAME"/>, anywhere on the map. The two sides only
	ever agree on the channel name, so a new charge or a new detonator can be
	added without touching the other; what a signalled object DOES is its own
	<remote action=>. The detonator is never consumed. impactMs, if present, is
	the delay between the press and the signal.

	<combat> describes a melee swing's hit area:
		offset  where the swing starts, relative to the player, before rotation
		range   how far forward it reaches
		radius  how far to the SIDE of the aim line it still connects. The
		        server sweeps a capsule of this half-width along the range, so a
		        target beside what you are pointing at is hit rather than only
		        one dead centre. Values match the client's own AI/weapon catalog
		        (client.js `radius`, paired there with `distance` = range).
		        Omit it and the swing falls back to a 15-unit hairline, which is
		        the old behaviour.
-->
